                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Mistakes Choosing Joint Sand
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    The choice between polymeric and regular sand is a maintenance decision more than a performance one, and it is usually made on the price of the bag alone.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Regular Sand for a High-Traffic Patio</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Ordinary joint sand washes out in heavy rain, blows out under a leaf blower, and offers no resistance to weeds or ants. On a driveway or a patio in regular use, that means topping up joints every season indefinitely.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Polymeric Sand Without the Time to Install It Properly</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Polymeric sand needs dry pavers, a completely swept surface, careful staged watering, and a dry day after. Rushed, it hazes the pavers and cures weak. If you cannot give it those conditions, ordinary sand installed well beats polymeric sand installed badly.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Polymeric Sand Is Permanent</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Even a good polymeric installation degrades over five to ten years, particularly at edges and in heavy traffic, and it needs topping up. It dramatically reduces maintenance rather than eliminating it, and expecting otherwise leads to disappointment around year six.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Drainage Requirements</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Polymeric sand seals joints tightly, which is exactly what you want on a well-drained base and a problem on an installation relying on joint infiltration to shed water. Check how your patio is meant to drain before locking the joints up.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    <strong class="text-white">Polymeric sand versus regular sand</strong> is really a question of how much ongoing maintenance you want. Polymeric sand resists washout, weeds, and ants, and asks for a careful installation and a dry window in return. Regular sand costs a fraction as much, installs in minutes, and needs topping up more or less annually.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    For a patio or driveway you want to leave alone, polymeric sand installed properly is the better long-term deal. For a low-traffic path, or a job you cannot give a dry day and a careful sweep, regular sand is an honest choice rather than a compromise.
                  </p>
                </div>

